# Assignment Submission

## Contents
| File | Task |
|---|---|
| `process-user.ts` | Fixed TypeScript function handling optional user/age |
| `UserProfile.tsx` | Reviewed React component with all issues fixed |
| `unpaid-bills.sql` | Query for homes with more than 10000 unpaid |

## Key decisions
- React: used AbortController to prevent race conditions and stale updates
- React: stored `onFetch` in a ref to avoid an infinite refetch loop
- SQL: grouped by `id` and `name` so homes with the same name stay separate

## Edge cases handled
- Network and HTTP errors (non-2xx responses)
- Rapid `userId` changes and unmounting mid-request
- Missing `user` or `age` in `processUser`


# Question 1 
function processUser(user?: { name: string; age?: number }) {
   const formattedAge = user.age.toFixed(2);
   console.log(user.name + ' is ' + formattedAge + ' years old');
 }
 processUser();
# Solution in Process.ts

# Question 2 
 import React, { useState, useEffect } from 'react';
 interface UserProps { userId: string; onFetch: (data: any) => void; }
 export const UserProfile: React.FC<UserProps> = ({ userId, onFetch }) => {
   const [user, setUser] = useState();
   useEffect(() => {
    fetch('/api/user/' + userId)
       .then((res) => res.json())
       .then((data) => {
         setUser(data);
        onFetch(data);
      });
  }, [userId, onFetch]);
  return <div>{user ? user.name : 'Loading...'}</div>;
 };

# Solution in UserProfile.tsx

